1
00:03:15,621 --> 00:03:17,830
You never did eat your lunch, did you?

2
00:03:17,915 --> 00:03:19,666
I better get back to the office.

3
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:22,835
These extended lunch hours
give my boss excess acid.

4
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:24,337
Why don't you call your boss and tell him

5
00:03:24,421 --> 00:03:26,756
you're taking the rest of the afternoon off?

6
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:29,217
Friday anyway, and hot.

7
00:03:29,510 --> 00:03:33,471
What do I do with my free afternoon?
Walk you to the airport?

8
00:03:34,431 --> 00:03:36,808
Well, we could laze around here a while longer.

9
00:03:36,892 --> 00:03:38,184
(MARlON SlGHS)

10
00:03:38,268 --> 00:03:40,520
MARlON: Checking-out time is 3:00 p.m.

11
00:03:41,021 --> 00:03:44,982
Hotels of this sort aren't interested
in you when you come in,

12
00:03:45,109 --> 00:03:47,110
but when your time is up...

13
00:03:47,194 --> 00:03:49,737
Oh, Sam, I hate having to be with you
in a place like this.

14
00:03:49,822 --> 00:03:51,531
I've heard of married couples
who deliberately spend

15
00:03:51,615 --> 00:03:52,782
an occasional night in a cheap hotel.

16
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,161
When you're married you can do
a lot of things deliberately.

17
00:03:57,621 --> 00:04:00,248
You sure talk like a girl who's been married.

18
00:04:00,332 --> 00:04:03,126
Oh, Sam, this is the last time.

19
00:04:03,544 --> 00:04:05,962
- Yeah? For what?
- For this.

20
00:04:07,005 --> 00:04:10,299
For meeting you in secret so we can be secretive.

21
00:04:10,926 --> 00:04:15,096
You come down here on business trips,
and we steal lunch hours.

22
00:04:16,181 --> 00:04:18,474
I wish you wouldn't even come.

23
00:04:19,184 --> 00:04:22,562
All right. What do we do instead?
Write each other lurid love letters?

24
00:04:22,646 --> 00:04:24,856
Oh, I have to go, Sam.

25
00:04:27,443 --> 00:04:29,986
- I can come down next week.
- No.

26
00:04:30,654 --> 00:04:34,991
Not even just to see you?
Have lunch, in public?

27
00:04:36,201 --> 00:04:39,495
Oh, we can see each other.
We can even have dinner.

28
00:04:40,539 --> 00:04:42,039
But respectably.

29
00:04:42,124 --> 00:04:44,917
In my house,
with my mother's picture on the mantel

30
00:04:45,002 --> 00:04:48,421
and my sister helping me broil
a big steak for three.

31
00:04:48,922 --> 00:04:50,423
And after the steak,

32
00:04:50,507 --> 00:04:53,092
do we send sister to the movies,
turn Mama's picture to the wall?

33
00:04:53,177 --> 00:04:54,260
Sam!

34
00:04:57,431 --> 00:04:58,806
All right.

35
00:05:05,522 --> 00:05:08,691
Marion, whenever it's possible, I want to see you.

36
00:05:08,776 --> 00:05:12,028
And under any circumstances, even respectability.

37
00:05:12,988 --> 00:05:16,282
You make respectability sound disrespectful.

38
00:05:16,366 --> 00:05:17,950
Oh, no, I'm all for it.

39
00:05:18,035 --> 00:05:21,621
It requires patience, temperance,
a lot of sweating out.

40
00:05:22,206 --> 00:05:24,957
Otherwise, though, it's just hard work.

41
00:05:26,084 --> 00:05:30,630
But if I can see you and touch you,
even as simply as this, I won't mind.

42
00:05:33,217 --> 00:05:35,718
I'm tired of sweating for people who aren't there.

43
00:05:35,969 --> 00:05:38,846
I sweat to pay off my father's debts,
and he's in his grave.

44
00:05:38,931 --> 00:05:40,139
I sweat to pay my ex-wife alimony,

45
00:05:40,224 --> 00:05:43,935
and she's living on the other side
of the world somewhere.

46
00:05:45,729 --> 00:05:47,146
I pay, too.

47
00:05:48,524 --> 00:05:51,234
They also pay who meet in hotel rooms.

48
00:05:52,444 --> 00:05:55,655
A couple of years and my debts will be paid off.

49
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:58,324
If she ever remarries, the alimony stops.

50
00:05:58,408 --> 00:06:02,787
-  haven't even been married once yet.
- Yeah. But when you do, you'll swing.

51
00:06:02,871 --> 00:06:04,997
Oh, Sam, let's get married.

52
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:14,423
Yeah. And live with me in a storeroom
behind a hardware store in Fairvale?

53
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:17,635
We'll have lots of laughs. I'll tell you what.

54
00:06:17,719 --> 00:06:21,347
When I send my ex-wife her alimony,
you can lick the stamps.

55
00:06:21,431 --> 00:06:23,266
I'll lick the stamps.

56
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:34,026
Marion, you want to cut this off,

57
00:06:34,111 --> 00:06:36,571
go out and find yourself somebody available?

58
00:06:36,655 --> 00:06:38,447
I'm thinking of it.

59
00:06:42,077 --> 00:06:45,037
How could you even think a thing like that?

60
00:06:45,706 --> 00:06:48,583
- Don't miss your plane.
- Hey, we can leave together, can't we?

61
00:06:48,667 --> 00:06:51,627
I'm late and you have to put your shoes on.

62
00:07:06,018 --> 00:07:07,768
Is Mr Lowery back from lunch?

63
00:07:07,853 --> 00:07:10,271
He's lunching with the man
who's buying the Harris Street property.

64
00:07:10,355 --> 00:07:14,275
You know, the oil lease man.
That's why he's late. You got a headache?

65
00:07:14,359 --> 00:07:15,818
It'll pass.

66
00:07:15,903 --> 00:07:17,486
Headaches are like resolutions,

67
00:07:17,571 --> 00:07:19,530
you forget them as soon as they stop hurting.

68
00:07:19,615 --> 00:07:22,700
Have you got some aspirin?
I've got something, not aspirin.

69
00:07:22,784 --> 00:07:25,453
My mother's doctor gave them to me
the day of my wedding.

70
00:07:25,537 --> 00:07:29,332
Teddy was furious when he found out
I'd taken tranquilisers.

71
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:31,334
Any calls?

72
00:07:31,793 --> 00:07:35,713
Teddy called me.
My mother called to see if Teddy called.

73
00:07:35,797 --> 00:07:38,591
Oh, your sister called to say
she's going to Tucson to do some buying

74
00:07:38,675 --> 00:07:39,884
and she'll be gone the whole weekend and...

75
00:07:39,968 --> 00:07:41,302
(HORN HONKlNG)

76
00:07:44,139 --> 00:07:45,806
Wow.

77
00:07:45,891 --> 00:07:48,017
It's as hot as fresh milk.

78
00:07:48,810 --> 00:07:53,481
Hey, you girls ought to get your boss
to air condition you up.

79
00:07:53,565 --> 00:07:54,941
He can afford it today.

80
00:07:55,025 --> 00:07:56,317
(CHUCKLES)

81
00:07:56,401 --> 00:07:58,527
Marion, will you get the copies
of that deed ready for Mr Cassidy?

82
00:07:58,612 --> 00:08:02,073
- Yes, sir.
- Tomorrow's the day, my sweet little girl.

83
00:08:02,866 --> 00:08:05,701
Oh, not you. My daughter.

84
00:08:06,453 --> 00:08:07,787
A baby.

85
00:08:08,789 --> 00:08:13,542
And tomorrow she stands her sweet self
up there and gets married away from me.

86
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:17,672
I want you to take a look at my baby.

87
00:08:19,216 --> 00:08:20,466
(CHUCKLES)

88
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:22,468
Eighteen years old

89
00:08:22,886 --> 00:08:26,722
and she never had an unhappy day
in any one of those years.

90
00:08:27,516 --> 00:08:29,767
Come on, Tom. My office is air conditioned.

91
00:08:29,851 --> 00:08:32,687
Do you know what I do about unhappiness?

92
00:08:33,605 --> 00:08:35,147
I buy it off.

93
00:08:35,565 --> 00:08:37,608
Are... Are you unhappy?

94
00:08:39,277 --> 00:08:40,987
Not inordinately.

95
00:08:42,823 --> 00:08:46,242
I'm buying this house
for my baby's wedding present.

96
00:08:47,452 --> 00:08:49,745
$40,000 cash.

97
00:08:51,623 --> 00:08:56,752
Now that's not buying happiness.
That's just buying off unhappiness.

98
00:08:57,129 --> 00:08:58,170
(CHUCKLES)

99
00:08:58,255 --> 00:08:59,255
(MONEY FLAPPlNG)

100
00:08:59,339 --> 00:09:02,383
I never carry more than I can afford to lose.

101
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,344
- CASSlDY: Count them!
- I declare!

102
00:09:05,429 --> 00:09:08,139
I don't. That's how I get to keep it.

103
00:09:08,974 --> 00:09:13,019
Tom, a cash transaction of this size is most irregular.

104
00:09:13,103 --> 00:09:16,230
So what? It's my private money.

105
00:09:16,314 --> 00:09:17,648
Now it's yours.

106
00:09:17,733 --> 00:09:18,983
Suppose we put it in the safe,

107
00:09:19,067 --> 00:09:22,570
and then Monday morning
when you're feeling good...

108
00:09:22,654 --> 00:09:24,822
Oh, speaking of feeling good,

109
00:09:24,906 --> 00:09:28,034
where's that bottle you said was in your desk?

110
00:09:28,118 --> 00:09:29,285
Uh-oh!

111
00:09:29,369 --> 00:09:31,078
(CHUCKLES)

112
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:36,417
You know, sometimes I can keep my mouth shut.

113
00:09:41,548 --> 00:09:44,300
Lowery, I am dying of thirst-aroonie.

114
00:09:48,472 --> 00:09:50,514
I don't even want it in the office over the weekend.

115
00:09:50,599 --> 00:09:52,808
Put it in the safe deposit box in the bank,

116
00:09:52,893 --> 00:09:54,977
and we'll get him to give us
a cheque on Monday instead.

117
00:09:55,062 --> 00:09:56,145
Yes.

118
00:10:01,693 --> 00:10:03,402
He was flirting with you.

119
00:10:03,487 --> 00:10:06,322
I guess he must've noticed my wedding ring.

120
00:10:19,878 --> 00:10:21,170
LOWERY: Come in.

121
00:10:23,507 --> 00:10:27,426
The copies. Mr Lowery, if you don't mind,
I'd like to go right on home after the bank.

122
00:10:27,511 --> 00:10:29,762
- I have a slight...
- You go right on home.

123
00:10:29,846 --> 00:10:31,931
Because me and your boss are going out

124
00:10:32,015 --> 00:10:34,558
and get ourselves a little drinking done. Right?

125
00:10:34,643 --> 00:10:37,770
-Of course. Do you feel ill?
-Just a headache.

126
00:10:37,854 --> 00:10:42,191
What you need is a weekend in Las Vegas,
the playground of the world.

127
00:10:42,275 --> 00:10:45,528
I'm going to spend this weekend in bed. Thank you.

128
00:10:48,365 --> 00:10:51,951
Aren't you going to take the pills?
They'll knock that headache out.

129
00:10:52,035 --> 00:10:54,411
Can't buy off unhappiness with pills.

130
00:10:54,496 --> 00:10:55,663
(SlGHS)

131
00:10:55,747 --> 00:11:00,459
I guess I'll go put this money in the bank
and then go home and sleep it off.

132
00:13:02,499 --> 00:13:05,751
(SAMSVOICE) Marion, what in the world...
What are you doing up here?

133
00:13:05,835 --> 00:13:08,712
Of course I'm glad to see you. I always am.

134
00:13:08,797 --> 00:13:10,589
What is it, Marion?

135
00:14:12,485 --> 00:14:13,777
(BRAKES SQUEAK)

136
00:14:48,772 --> 00:14:49,730
(STARTS ENGlNE)

137
00:14:49,814 --> 00:14:51,398
Hold it there.

138
00:14:57,447 --> 00:14:59,114
In quite a hurry.

139
00:14:59,532 --> 00:15:02,284
Yes. I didn't intend to sleep so long.

140
00:15:02,369 --> 00:15:05,287
I almost had an accident last night from sleepiness,

141
00:15:05,372 --> 00:15:08,957
- so I decided to pull over.
- You slept here all night?

142
00:15:09,793 --> 00:15:12,795
Yes. As I said, I couldn't keep my eyes open.

143
00:15:13,171 --> 00:15:16,548
There are plenty of motels in this area.
You should've...

144
00:15:16,633 --> 00:15:18,384
I mean, just to be safe.

145
00:15:18,468 --> 00:15:23,305
I didn't intend to sleep all night.
I just pulled over. Have I broken any laws?

146
00:15:23,848 --> 00:15:26,266
- No, ma'am.
- Then I'm free to go?

147
00:15:27,936 --> 00:15:29,728
Is anything wrong?

148
00:15:30,814 --> 00:15:34,525
Of course not.
Am I acting as if there's something wrong?

149
00:15:34,609 --> 00:15:38,070
- Frankly, yes.
- Please, I'd like to go.

150
00:15:38,988 --> 00:15:41,824
- Well, is there?
- Is there what?

151
00:15:44,619 --> 00:15:46,537
I've told you there's nothing wrong,

152
00:15:46,621 --> 00:15:49,540
except that I'm in a hurry
and you're taking up my time.

153
00:15:49,624 --> 00:15:50,916
(STARTS ENGlNE)

154
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,669
Now, just a moment. Turn your motor off, please.

155
00:15:53,837 --> 00:15:55,254
(TURNS OFF ENGlNE)

156
00:15:55,338 --> 00:15:57,339
May I see your licence?

157
00:16:00,093 --> 00:16:02,219
- Why?
- Please.

158
00:16:39,507 --> 00:16:40,799
(STARTS ENGlNE)

159
00:18:08,805 --> 00:18:10,806
Be with you in a second!

160
00:19:14,621 --> 00:19:16,788
- I'm in no mood for trouble.
- What?

161
00:19:16,873 --> 00:19:19,791
There's an old saying. "The first customer
of the day is always the most trouble."

162
00:19:19,876 --> 00:19:21,501
But like I say, I'm in no mood for it,

163
00:19:21,586 --> 00:19:24,880
so I'm gonna treat you so fair and square
that you won't have one human reason...

164
00:19:24,964 --> 00:19:27,132
Can I trade my car in and take another?

165
00:19:27,216 --> 00:19:31,345
Do anything you have a mind to.
Being a woman, you will. That yours?

166
00:19:31,429 --> 00:19:33,722
Yes, it's... There's nothing wrong with it.

167
00:19:33,806 --> 00:19:36,308
- I just...
- Sick of the sight of it.

168
00:19:36,392 --> 00:19:37,517
Why don't you have a look around here

169
00:19:37,602 --> 00:19:39,019
and see if there's something that strikes your eyes.

170
00:19:39,103 --> 00:19:41,104
And meanwhile, I'll have my mechanic
give yours the once-over.

171
00:19:41,189 --> 00:19:43,148
- You want some coffee? I was just about...
- No, thank you.

172
00:19:43,232 --> 00:19:45,609
I'm in a hurry and I just wanna make a change.

173
00:19:45,693 --> 00:19:47,152
One thing people never ought to be

174
00:19:47,236 --> 00:19:49,196
when they're buying used cars and that's in a hurry.

175
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:53,200
But like I said, it's too nice a day to argue.
I'll shoot your car in the garage here.

176
00:19:56,537 --> 00:19:57,496
(CAR DOOR CLOSES)

177
00:19:57,580 --> 00:19:58,914
(STARTS ENGlNE)

178
00:20:15,682 --> 00:20:17,683
That's the one I'd have picked for you myself.

179
00:20:17,767 --> 00:20:18,892
- How much?
- Go ahead.

180
00:20:18,976 --> 00:20:21,478
- Spin it around the block.
- It looks fine.

181
00:20:21,562 --> 00:20:23,522
How much would it be with my car?

182
00:20:23,606 --> 00:20:27,693
You mean you don't want the usual day
and a half to think it over?

183
00:20:27,777 --> 00:20:30,737
You are in a hurry, aren't you?
Somebody chasing you?

184
00:20:30,822 --> 00:20:32,781
Of course not. Please.

185
00:20:33,157 --> 00:20:37,577
Well, it's the first time the customer
ever high-pressured the salesman.

186
00:20:39,038 --> 00:20:40,789
I figure roughly

187
00:20:43,376 --> 00:20:45,502
your car plus $700.

188
00:20:46,504 --> 00:20:50,132
- $700.
- You always got time to argue money, huh?

189
00:20:51,342 --> 00:20:52,718
All right.

190
00:20:58,099 --> 00:21:00,225
I take it you can prove that car is yours.

191
00:21:00,309 --> 00:21:03,270
I mean, out-of-state licence and all.
You got your pink slip...

192
00:21:03,354 --> 00:21:07,149
I believe I have the necessary papers.
Is there a ladies' room?

193
00:21:07,233 --> 00:21:08,859
In the building.

194
00:21:20,079 --> 00:21:21,538
Over there.

195
00:22:18,513 --> 00:22:21,014
I think you better take it for a trial spin.

196
00:22:21,098 --> 00:22:23,683
I don't want any bad word of mouth
about California Charlie.

197
00:22:23,768 --> 00:22:26,770
I'd really rather not.
Can't we just settle this and...

198
00:22:26,854 --> 00:22:29,397
I might as well be perfectly honest with you, ma'am.

199
00:22:29,482 --> 00:22:32,192
- It's not that I don't trust you, but...
- But what?

200
00:22:32,276 --> 00:22:34,110
Is there anything so terribly wrong

201
00:22:34,195 --> 00:22:37,155
about making a decision and wanting to hurry?

202
00:22:37,406 --> 00:22:40,408
- Do you think I've stolen my car?
- No, ma'am.

203
00:22:41,494 --> 00:22:43,745
All right, let's go inside.

204
00:22:51,587 --> 00:22:52,879
(STARTS ENGlNE)

205
00:23:20,783 --> 00:23:21,992
MAN: Hey!

206
00:23:29,208 --> 00:23:31,501
Just put it in here, please.

207
00:23:34,463 --> 00:23:35,881
Thank you.

208
00:23:45,057 --> 00:23:46,057
CALlFORNlA CHARLlE'S VOlCE: Heck, Officer,

209
00:23:46,142 --> 00:23:47,350
that was the first time I ever saw the customer

210
00:23:47,435 --> 00:23:49,644
high-pressure the salesman.

211
00:23:49,729 --> 00:23:51,605
Somebody chasing her?

212
00:23:51,689 --> 00:23:54,733
OFFlCER: I betterhave a look at those papers, Charlie.

213
00:23:54,817 --> 00:23:56,985
CALlFORNlA CHARLlE:
She look like a wrong one to you?

214
00:23:57,069 --> 00:23:58,320
OFFlCER: acted like one.

215
00:23:59,238 --> 00:24:02,365
CALlFORNlA CHARLlE: The only funny thing,
she paid me $700 in cash.

216
00:24:12,376 --> 00:24:16,212
CAROLlNE: Yes, Mr Lowery.
LOWERY: Caroline, Marion still isn't in?

217
00:24:16,297 --> 00:24:19,758
CAROLlNE: No, Mr Lowery, but then
she's always a bit late on Monday mornings.

218
00:24:19,842 --> 00:24:21,384
LOWERY: Buzz me the minute she comes in.

219
00:24:24,305 --> 00:24:26,765
And call her sister.
no one's answering at the house.

220
00:24:26,849 --> 00:24:29,225
CAROLINE: I called her sister, Mr Lowery,
where she works,

221
00:24:29,310 --> 00:24:31,311
the Music Makers Music Store, you know,

222
00:24:31,395 --> 00:24:34,022
and she doesn't know where Marion is
any more than we do.

223
00:24:34,106 --> 00:24:35,273
LOWERY: You better run out to the house.

224
00:24:35,358 --> 00:24:37,901
She may be, well, unable to answer the phone.

225
00:24:37,985 --> 00:24:40,862
CAROLlNE: Hersister's going to do that.
She's as worried as we are.

226
00:24:45,326 --> 00:24:47,285
LOWERY: No, I haven't the faintest idea.

227
00:24:47,370 --> 00:24:50,288
As I said, I last saw your sister
when she left this office on Friday.

228
00:24:50,373 --> 00:24:53,917
She said she didn't feel well and wanted
to leave early, and I said she could.

229
00:24:54,001 --> 00:24:57,003
That was the last I saw... Oh, wait a minute.

230
00:24:57,088 --> 00:24:59,923
I did see her some time later, driving...

231
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:04,386
I think you'd better come over here
to my office, quick.

232
00:25:04,595 --> 00:25:07,055
Caroline, get Mr Cassidy for me.

233
00:25:09,892 --> 00:25:13,353
After all, Cassidy, I told you, all that cash!

234
00:25:13,854 --> 00:25:16,022
I'm not taking the responsibility.

235
00:25:16,107 --> 00:25:17,649
Oh, for heaven's sake.

236
00:25:17,733 --> 00:25:20,694
A girl works for you for 10 years, you trust her.

237
00:25:20,778 --> 00:25:22,737
All right, yes, you better come over.

238
00:25:25,157 --> 00:25:28,201
CASSIDY: Well, I ain't about to kiss off $40,000!

239
00:25:28,285 --> 00:25:32,330
I'll get it back, and if any of it's missing,
I'll replace it with her fine, soft flesh!

240
00:25:32,415 --> 00:25:35,333
- I'll track her, never you doubt it.
- LOWERY: Hold on, Cassidy.

241
00:25:35,418 --> 00:25:40,839
I still can't believe...
It must be some kind of a mystery. I can't...

242
00:25:40,923 --> 00:25:44,134
You checked with the bank, no?
They never laid eyes on her, no?

243
00:25:44,218 --> 00:25:48,221
You still trusting? Hot creepers!
She sat there while I dumped it out!

244
00:25:48,305 --> 00:25:52,767
Hardly even looked at it. Planning.
And even flirting with me!

245
00:27:50,511 --> 00:27:57,600
(HONKlNG HORN)

246
00:28:18,080 --> 00:28:20,915
Gee, I'm sorry I didn't hear you in all this rain.

247
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:22,792
Go ahead in, please.

248
00:28:28,257 --> 00:28:30,925
- Dirty night.
- You have a vacancy?

249
00:28:31,010 --> 00:28:34,929
Oh, we have 1 2 vacancies.
Twelve cabins, 1 2 vacancies.

250
00:28:35,347 --> 00:28:37,932
They... They moved away the highway.

251
00:28:38,601 --> 00:28:41,603
Oh, I thought I'd gotten off the main road.

252
00:28:42,730 --> 00:28:43,855
I knew you must have.

253
00:28:43,939 --> 00:28:47,483
Nobody ever stops here any more
unless they've done that.

254
00:28:47,860 --> 00:28:51,863
But there's no sense dwelling on our losses.

255
00:28:51,947 --> 00:28:56,117
We just keep on lighting the lights
and following the formalities.

256
00:29:00,122 --> 00:29:03,124
Your home address.
Oh, just the town will do.

257
00:29:07,379 --> 00:29:08,838
Los Angeles.

258
00:29:16,305 --> 00:29:18,723
Cabin One.
It's closer in case you want anything.

259
00:29:18,807 --> 00:29:20,016
Right next to the office.

260
00:29:20,100 --> 00:29:23,061
I want sleep more than anything else,
except maybe food.

261
00:29:23,145 --> 00:29:26,898
Well, there's a big diner about 1 0 miles
up the road, just outside of Fairvale.

262
00:29:26,982 --> 00:29:31,152
- Am I that close to Fairvale?
- Fifteen miles. I'll get your bags.

263
00:29:42,790 --> 00:29:44,165
(UNLOCKlNG DOOR)

264
00:29:50,297 --> 00:29:52,340
Boy, it's stuffy in here.

265
00:29:58,013 --> 00:30:00,598
Well, the mattress is soft

266
00:30:01,433 --> 00:30:05,311
and there's hangers in the closet and
stationery with Bates Motel printed on it

267
00:30:05,396 --> 00:30:08,773
in case you wanna make
your friends back home feel envious.

268
00:30:08,857 --> 00:30:10,358
And the...

269
00:30:12,778 --> 00:30:15,113
- Over there.
- The bathroom.

270
00:30:15,614 --> 00:30:16,739
Yeah.

271
00:30:17,658 --> 00:30:21,828
Well, if you want anything, just tap on the wall.

272
00:30:21,912 --> 00:30:24,205
- I'll be in the office.
- Thank you, Mr Bates.

273
00:30:24,290 --> 00:30:25,873
Norman Bates.

274
00:30:29,545 --> 00:30:32,797
You're not really gonna go out again
and drive up to the diner, are you?

275
00:30:32,881 --> 00:30:34,132
No.

276
00:30:34,216 --> 00:30:37,135
Well, then would you do me a favour?
Would you have dinner with me?

277
00:30:37,219 --> 00:30:38,678
I was just about to myself.

278
00:30:38,762 --> 00:30:41,097
You know, nothing special,
just sandwiches and milk.

279
00:30:41,181 --> 00:30:44,851
But I'd like it very much
if you'd come up to the house.

280
00:30:46,061 --> 00:30:49,731
I don't set a fancy table,
but the kitchen's awful homey.

281
00:30:50,482 --> 00:30:52,233
- I'd like to.
- All right.

282
00:30:52,318 --> 00:30:54,986
You get yourself settled and take off your wet shoes,

283
00:30:55,070 --> 00:30:56,821
and I'll be back as soon as it's ready.

284
00:30:56,905 --> 00:30:58,114
Okay.

285
00:30:58,741 --> 00:31:00,491
With my... With my trusty umbrella.

286
00:31:00,576 --> 00:31:01,576
(LAUGHS)

287
00:32:22,658 --> 00:32:25,243
MRS. BATES: No! I tell you no!

288
00:32:25,327 --> 00:32:28,621
I won't have you bringing
strange young girls in for supper!

289
00:32:28,705 --> 00:32:32,041
By candlelight, I suppose,
in the cheap, erotic fashion

290
00:32:32,126 --> 00:32:34,460
of young men with cheap, erotic minds!

291
00:32:34,545 --> 00:32:36,170
NORMAN: Mother, please.

292
00:32:36,422 --> 00:32:39,424
MRS. BATES: And then what, after supper?
Music? Whispers?

293
00:32:39,508 --> 00:32:42,802
NORMAN: Mother, she's just a stranger.
She's hungry and it's raining out.

294
00:32:42,886 --> 00:32:48,266
MRS. BATES: "Mother, she's just a stranger."
As if men don't desire strangers. As if...

295
00:32:48,350 --> 00:32:52,562
(SHUDDERlNG) I refuse to speak of
disgusting things, because they disgust me!

296
00:32:52,729 --> 00:32:54,939
You understand, boy? Go on.

297
00:32:55,190 --> 00:32:58,192
Go tell her she'll not be appeasing her ugly appetite

298
00:32:58,277 --> 00:33:00,695
with my food or my son!

299
00:33:01,113 --> 00:33:04,115
Or do I have to tell her
'cause you don't have the guts?

300
00:33:04,199 --> 00:33:07,785
- Huh, boy? You have the guts, boy?
- NORMAN: Shut up! Shut up!

301
00:33:11,331 --> 00:33:12,498
(DOOR OPENS)

302
00:33:13,250 --> 00:33:14,625
(DOOR CLOSES)

303
00:33:49,953 --> 00:33:52,747
- I've caused you some trouble.
- No.

304
00:33:54,082 --> 00:33:56,250
Mother... My mother...

305
00:33:56,919 --> 00:33:58,753
What is the phrase?

306
00:33:59,671 --> 00:34:01,923
She isn't quite herself today.

307
00:34:02,758 --> 00:34:06,552
You shouldn't have bothered.
I really don't have that much of an appetite.

308
00:34:08,555 --> 00:34:10,097
Oh, I'm sorry.

309
00:34:12,726 --> 00:34:15,728
I wish you could apologise for other people.

310
00:34:16,355 --> 00:34:18,064
Don't worry about it.

311
00:34:18,148 --> 00:34:21,734
But as long as you've fixed a supper,
we may as well eat it.

312
00:34:32,287 --> 00:34:36,791
It might be nicer and warmer in the office.

313
00:34:51,139 --> 00:34:52,932
Well, it stopped raining.

314
00:34:53,016 --> 00:34:55,560
Eating in an office is just too officious.

315
00:34:55,644 --> 00:34:56,727
(CHUCKLES)

316
00:34:56,812 --> 00:34:59,522
- I have the parlour back here.
- All right.

317
00:35:15,122 --> 00:35:17,665
- Sit down.
- Thank you.

318
00:35:21,003 --> 00:35:22,837
You're very kind.

319
00:35:24,172 --> 00:35:27,174
It's all for you. I'm not hungry. Go ahead.

320
00:35:33,849 --> 00:35:36,017
You... You eat like a bird.

321
00:35:37,561 --> 00:35:39,604
And you'd know, of course.

322
00:35:40,355 --> 00:35:41,897
No, not really.

323
00:35:42,482 --> 00:35:45,359
Anyway, I hear the expression
"Eats like a bird"

324
00:35:45,444 --> 00:35:49,530
is really a false... Falsity.

325
00:35:50,449 --> 00:35:53,326
Because birds really eat a tremendous lot.

326
00:35:56,163 --> 00:35:59,498
But I don't really know anything about birds.

327
00:35:59,583 --> 00:36:01,417
My hobby is stuffing things.

328
00:36:01,501 --> 00:36:03,336
You know, taxidermy.

329
00:36:04,171 --> 00:36:07,381
And I guess I'd just rather stuff birds because

330
00:36:08,008 --> 00:36:10,635
I hate the look of beasts when they're stuffed.

331
00:36:10,719 --> 00:36:12,678
You know, foxes and chimps.

332
00:36:12,763 --> 00:36:16,682
Some people even stuff dogs and cats,
but, oh, I can't do that.

333
00:36:17,267 --> 00:36:19,685
I think only birds look well stuffed because...

334
00:36:19,770 --> 00:36:23,230
Well, because they're kind of passive to begin with.

335
00:36:23,899 --> 00:36:26,692
It's a strange hobby. Curious.

336
00:36:27,402 --> 00:36:30,154
- Uncommon, too.
- Oh, I imagine so.

337
00:36:30,781 --> 00:36:34,241
And it's not as expensive as you'd think.
It's cheap, really.

338
00:36:34,326 --> 00:36:36,869
You know, needles and thread, sawdust.

339
00:36:36,953 --> 00:36:39,705
The chemicals are the only thing that cost anything.

340
00:36:39,790 --> 00:36:41,874
A man should have a hobby.

341
00:36:42,584 --> 00:36:46,462
Well, it's... It's more than a hobby.

342
00:36:48,632 --> 00:36:51,842
A hobby's supposed to pass the time, not fill it.

343
00:36:52,636 --> 00:36:54,553
Is your time so empty?

344
00:36:54,638 --> 00:36:55,763
No.

345
00:36:57,474 --> 00:36:59,725
Well, I run the office

346
00:36:59,810 --> 00:37:02,228
and tend the cabins and grounds

347
00:37:02,312 --> 00:37:05,022
and do little errands for my mother.

348
00:37:05,107 --> 00:37:08,442
The ones she allows I might be capable of doing.

349
00:37:09,027 --> 00:37:11,153
Do you go out with friends?

350
00:37:14,116 --> 00:37:17,034
Well, a boy's best friend is his mother.

351
00:37:19,329 --> 00:37:23,249
You've never had an empty moment
in your entire life, have you?

352
00:37:25,293 --> 00:37:28,462
- Only my share.
- Where are you going?

353
00:37:31,425 --> 00:37:33,342
I didn't mean to pry.

354
00:37:33,427 --> 00:37:35,845
I'm looking for a private island.

355
00:37:37,222 --> 00:37:39,598
What are you running away from?

356
00:37:42,144 --> 00:37:44,186
- Why do you ask that?
- I don't know.

357
00:37:46,148 --> 00:37:48,774
People never run away from anything.

358
00:37:51,486 --> 00:37:55,114
The rain didn't last long, did it?
You know what I think?

359
00:37:56,324 --> 00:37:57,908
I think that

360
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:02,621
we're all in our private traps,

361
00:38:03,331 --> 00:38:06,667
clamped in them,
and none of us can ever get out.

362
00:38:09,045 --> 00:38:11,255
We scratch and claw,

363
00:38:12,048 --> 00:38:14,884
but only at the air, only at each other.

364
00:38:15,761 --> 00:38:19,346
And for all of it, we never budge an inch.

365
00:38:20,515 --> 00:38:23,726
Sometimes we deliberately step into those traps.

366
00:38:26,062 --> 00:38:29,148
I was born in mine. I don't mind it any more.

367
00:38:29,232 --> 00:38:31,901
Oh, but you should. You should mind it.

368
00:38:31,985 --> 00:38:34,195
Oh, I do, but I say I don't.

369
00:38:37,407 --> 00:38:41,368
You know, if anyone ever
talked to me the way I heard,

370
00:38:42,037 --> 00:38:44,205
the way she spoke to you...

371
00:38:46,082 --> 00:38:49,001
Sometimes when she talks to me like that,

372
00:38:49,586 --> 00:38:54,048
I feel I'd like to go up there
and curse her and leave her forever.

373
00:38:54,716 --> 00:38:56,550
Or at least defy her.

374
00:39:01,223 --> 00:39:02,973
But I know I can't.

375
00:39:04,684 --> 00:39:06,060
She's ill.

376
00:39:06,561 --> 00:39:08,395
She sounded strong.

377
00:39:09,523 --> 00:39:11,232
No, I mean ill.

378
00:39:13,485 --> 00:39:17,154
She had to raise me all by herself,
after my father died.

379
00:39:17,239 --> 00:39:20,825
I was only five and it must've been
quite a strain for her.

380
00:39:20,909 --> 00:39:23,369
I mean, she didn't have to go to work
or anything like that.

381
00:39:23,453 --> 00:39:25,579
He left her a little money.

382
00:39:26,039 --> 00:39:29,917
Anyway, a few years ago, Mother met this man.

383
00:39:30,502 --> 00:39:33,754
And he talked her into building this motel.

384
00:39:33,839 --> 00:39:36,173
He could have talked her into anything.

385
00:39:36,258 --> 00:39:40,511
And when he died, too,
it was just too great a shock for her.

386
00:39:41,930 --> 00:39:43,848
And the way he died...

387
00:39:45,100 --> 00:39:46,892
(CHUCKLES)

388
00:39:47,269 --> 00:39:50,521
I guess it's nothing to talk about while you're eating.

389
00:39:50,605 --> 00:39:55,067
Anyway, it was just too great a loss for her.
She had nothing left.

390
00:39:56,778 --> 00:39:58,237
Except you.

391
00:39:59,698 --> 00:40:02,783
Well, a son is a poor substitute for a lover.

392
00:40:03,743 --> 00:40:05,619
Why don't you go away?

393
00:40:07,163 --> 00:40:09,456
To a private island, like you?

394
00:40:11,793 --> 00:40:12,877
(SlGHS)

395
00:40:12,961 --> 00:40:15,462
No, not like me.

396
00:40:16,506 --> 00:40:19,633
I couldn't do that. Who'd look after her?

397
00:40:21,177 --> 00:40:23,262
She'd be alone up there.

398
00:40:24,139 --> 00:40:26,056
Her fire would go out.

399
00:40:26,808 --> 00:40:29,310
It'd be cold and damp like a grave.

400
00:40:30,896 --> 00:40:32,646
If you love someone,

401
00:40:32,731 --> 00:40:35,983
you don't do that to them, even if you hate them.

402
00:40:36,985 --> 00:40:39,528
You understand, I don't hate her.

403
00:40:41,406 --> 00:40:43,449
I hate what she's become.

404
00:40:44,451 --> 00:40:46,243
I hate the illness.

405
00:40:47,662 --> 00:40:50,372
Wouldn't it be better if you put her

406
00:40:52,083 --> 00:40:53,667
someplace...

407
00:40:56,963 --> 00:41:00,049
You mean an institution? A madhouse?

408
00:41:04,179 --> 00:41:07,681
People always call a madhouse
"someplace," don't they?

409
00:41:07,766 --> 00:41:09,850
Put her in someplace.

410
00:41:11,353 --> 00:41:14,396
I'm sorry.
I didn't mean it to sound uncaring.

411
00:41:15,315 --> 00:41:17,733
What do you know about caring?

412
00:41:17,817 --> 00:41:20,945
Have you ever seen the inside
of one of those places?

413
00:41:21,029 --> 00:41:23,280
The laughing and the tears

414
00:41:24,032 --> 00:41:26,825
and the cruel eyes studying you.

415
00:41:26,910 --> 00:41:28,619
My mother there?

416
00:41:30,830 --> 00:41:32,706
(CHUCKLES) But she's harmless.

417
00:41:35,335 --> 00:41:38,545
She's as harmless as one of those stuffed birds.

418
00:41:39,714 --> 00:41:41,048
I am sorry.

419
00:41:41,883 --> 00:41:45,844
I only felt... It seems she's hurting you.

420
00:41:47,514 --> 00:41:50,474
- I meant well.
- People always mean well.

421
00:41:51,726 --> 00:41:54,019
They cluck their thick tongues and shake their heads

422
00:41:54,104 --> 00:41:56,730
and suggest, oh, so very delicately.

423
00:42:04,155 --> 00:42:06,740
Of course, I've suggested it myself.

424
00:42:08,410 --> 00:42:10,869
But I hate to even think about it.

425
00:42:11,621 --> 00:42:13,122
She needs me.

426
00:42:17,335 --> 00:42:19,503
It's not as if she were

427
00:42:19,587 --> 00:42:22,381
a maniac, a raving thing.

428
00:42:24,926 --> 00:42:27,845
She just goes a little mad sometimes.

429
00:42:30,890 --> 00:42:33,308
We all go a little mad sometimes.

430
00:42:36,021 --> 00:42:37,438
Haven't you?

431
00:42:39,566 --> 00:42:40,649
Yes.

432
00:42:41,818 --> 00:42:44,653
Sometimes just one time can be enough.

433
00:42:46,448 --> 00:42:47,781
Thank you.

434
00:42:48,825 --> 00:42:50,951
Thank you, Norman.

435
00:42:51,036 --> 00:42:52,286
Norman.

436
00:42:53,580 --> 00:42:57,416
Oh, you're not going back to your room already?

437
00:42:57,917 --> 00:42:59,376
I'm very tired.

438
00:42:59,461 --> 00:43:03,297
And I have a long drive tomorrow,
all the way back to Phoenix.

439
00:43:03,673 --> 00:43:04,965
Really?

440
00:43:05,884 --> 00:43:08,802
I stepped into a private trap back there

441
00:43:09,512 --> 00:43:13,265
and I'd like to go back
and try to pull myself out of it

442
00:43:15,060 --> 00:43:16,560
before it's too late for me, too.

443
00:43:16,644 --> 00:43:19,438
Are you sure you wouldn't like
to stay just a little while longer?

444
00:43:19,522 --> 00:43:21,440
Just for talk?

445
00:43:21,900 --> 00:43:23,734
Oh, I'd like to, but...

446
00:43:23,818 --> 00:43:25,069
All right.

447
00:43:25,153 --> 00:43:28,781
Well, I'll see you in the morning.
I'll bring you some breakfast, all right?

448
00:43:28,865 --> 00:43:31,658
-What time?
-Very early. Dawn.

449
00:43:32,243 --> 00:43:33,702
All right, Miss...

450
00:43:33,787 --> 00:43:36,080
- Crane.
- Crane. That's it.

451
00:43:36,164 --> 00:43:37,623
Good night.

452
00:43:46,049 --> 00:43:47,216
(DOOR OPENS)

453
00:43:48,718 --> 00:43:49,968
(DOOR CLOSES)

454
00:44:13,493 --> 00:44:15,661
(RUMMAGlNG)

455
00:46:01,476 --> 00:46:02,809
(GLASS CLlNKlNG)

456
00:46:11,986 --> 00:46:58,740
(SlGHS)

457
00:46:59,659 --> 00:47:01,743
(UNWRAPPlNG SOAP)

458
00:47:43,244 --> 00:47:44,578
(SCREAMlNG)

459
00:47:45,246 --> 00:47:46,496
No!

460
00:47:47,165 --> 00:47:48,999
(SCREAMlNG)

461
00:47:49,083 --> 00:47:50,167
No!

462
00:47:51,127 --> 00:47:52,669
(CONTlNUES SCREAMlNG)

463
00:47:57,925 --> 00:47:59,634
(HlGH-PlTCHED SCREAMlNG)

464
00:48:09,562 --> 00:48:11,146
(PANTlNG)

465
00:49:13,334 --> 00:49:15,210
(SHOWER CONTlNUES RUNNlNG)

466
00:49:53,040 --> 00:49:55,417
NORMAN: Mother! Oh, God, Mother!

467
00:49:55,501 --> 00:49:57,377
Blood! Blood!

468
00:50:38,920 --> 00:51:28,301
(SHOWER CONTlNUES RUNNlNG)

469
00:54:48,627 --> 00:54:50,170
(STARTS ENGlNE)

470
00:57:31,707 --> 00:57:33,791
(CAR PASSlNG BY)

471
00:58:22,216 --> 00:58:23,508
(STARTS ENGlNE)

472
00:58:58,085 --> 00:58:59,502
(TURNS ENGlNE OFF)

473
00:59:20,232 --> 00:59:21,733
(SLOSHlNG)

474
00:59:23,944 --> 00:59:25,445
(GURGLlNG)

475
00:59:27,865 --> 00:59:29,824
(GURGLlNG CONTlNUES)

476
01:00:05,069 --> 01:00:09,363
(AlR BUBBLES POPPlNG)

477
01:00:25,089 --> 01:00:27,006
WOMAN: I've tried many brands.

478
01:00:27,674 --> 01:00:31,677
So far, of those I've used,
I haven't had much luck with any of them.

479
01:00:31,762 --> 01:00:34,097
Let's see what they say about this one.

480
01:00:34,181 --> 01:00:36,474
They tell you what its ingredients are

481
01:00:36,558 --> 01:00:40,645
and how it's guaranteed to exterminate
every insect in the world,

482
01:00:41,188 --> 01:00:43,773
but they do not tell you
whether or not it's painless.

483
01:00:43,857 --> 01:00:47,568
And I say, insect or man,
death should always be painless.

484
01:00:48,362 --> 01:00:53,533
I suppose, this one seems to claim more
and better qualities than lots of the others.

485
01:01:04,378 --> 01:01:05,545
Sam?

486
01:01:06,046 --> 01:01:08,548
Sam! Lady wants to see you.

487
01:01:14,888 --> 01:01:17,306
- Yes, miss?
- I'm Marion's sister.

488
01:01:17,891 --> 01:01:20,434
- Oh, sure. Lila.
- Is Marion here?

489
01:01:22,146 --> 01:01:23,563
- Well, of course not.
- WOMAN: Thank you.

490
01:01:23,647 --> 01:01:25,398
Something wrong?

491
01:01:25,524 --> 01:01:27,066
(CASH REGlSTER RlNGS)

492
01:01:28,610 --> 01:01:30,403
She left home on Friday.

493
01:01:30,487 --> 01:01:34,157
I was in Tucson over the weekend
and I haven't heard from her since.

494
01:01:34,241 --> 01:01:36,117
Not even a phone call.

495
01:01:36,743 --> 01:01:39,829
Look, if you two are in this thing together,
I don't care, it's none of my business,

496
01:01:39,913 --> 01:01:41,998
but I want to talk to Marion
and I want her to tell me

497
01:01:42,082 --> 01:01:43,666
it's none of my business and then I'll go...

498
01:01:43,750 --> 01:01:47,044
Bob, run out and get yourself some lunch, will you?

499
01:01:47,129 --> 01:01:51,090
- That's okay, Sam. I brought it with me.
- Run out and eat it.

500
01:01:55,888 --> 01:01:58,806
SAM: Now, what thing could we be in together?

501
01:01:59,224 --> 01:02:01,142
LlLA: Sorry about the tears.

502
01:02:01,852 --> 01:02:04,604
Well, is Marion in trouble? What is it?

503
01:02:05,022 --> 01:02:07,773
MAN: Let's all talk about Marion, shall we?

504
01:02:11,445 --> 01:02:13,404
Who are you, friend?

505
01:02:13,488 --> 01:02:15,698
My name is Arbogast, friend.

506
01:02:15,782 --> 01:02:17,909
I'm a private investigator.

507
01:02:18,577 --> 01:02:21,829
- Where is she, Miss Crane?
- I don't know you.

508
01:02:22,289 --> 01:02:26,209
Why, I know you don't, because if you did,
I wouldn't be able to follow you.

509
01:02:26,293 --> 01:02:28,502
What's your interest in this?

510
01:02:29,046 --> 01:02:31,547
Well, $40,000.

511
01:02:33,300 --> 01:02:35,218
-$40,000?
-That's right.

512
01:02:38,972 --> 01:02:41,349
Well, one of you'd better tell me
what's going on, and tell me fast.

513
01:02:41,433 --> 01:02:43,643
- I can take just so much of this...
- Now, take it easy, friend.

514
01:02:43,727 --> 01:02:48,314
Take it easy.
It's just that your girlfriend stole $40,000.

515
01:02:48,398 --> 01:02:50,775
What are you talking about? What is this?

516
01:02:50,859 --> 01:02:55,029
She was supposed to bank it on Friday
for her boss and she didn't.

517
01:02:55,405 --> 01:02:58,741
- And no one has seen her since.
- Someone has seen her.

518
01:02:58,825 --> 01:03:01,869
Someone always sees a girl with $40,000.

519
01:03:01,954 --> 01:03:04,705
Sam, they don't want to prosecute,
they just want the money back.

520
01:03:04,790 --> 01:03:07,750
- Sam, if she's here...
- She isn't. She isn't.

521
01:03:07,834 --> 01:03:09,502
Miss Crane, can I ask you a question?

522
01:03:09,586 --> 01:03:12,338
Did you come up here on just a hunch
and nothing more?

523
01:03:12,422 --> 01:03:14,632
Not even a hunch. Just hope.

524
01:03:14,716 --> 01:03:18,302
Well, with a little checking I could get to believe you.

525
01:03:18,679 --> 01:03:21,180
I don't care if you believe me or not.

526
01:03:21,265 --> 01:03:24,600
All I want to do is see Marion
before she gets in this too deeply.

527
01:03:24,685 --> 01:03:28,145
Did you check in Phoenix? Hospitals?
Maybe she had an accident, or a holdup.

528
01:03:28,230 --> 01:03:30,106
No, she was seen leaving town in her own car,

529
01:03:30,190 --> 01:03:32,483
by her employer, I might add.

530
01:03:32,901 --> 01:03:35,194
I can't believe it. Can you?

531
01:03:37,030 --> 01:03:39,407
Well, you know,
we're always quickest to doubt people

532
01:03:39,491 --> 01:03:41,951
who have a reputation for being honest.

533
01:03:42,035 --> 01:03:44,328
I think she's here, Miss Crane.

534
01:03:44,413 --> 01:03:46,580
Where there's a boyfriend...

535
01:03:47,040 --> 01:03:48,874
Well, she's not back there with the nuts and bolts,

536
01:03:48,959 --> 01:03:51,711
but she's here, in this town somewhere.

537
01:03:52,170 --> 01:03:54,672
I'll find her. I'll be seeing you.

538
01:04:03,307 --> 01:04:06,392
(lNAUDlBLE)

539
01:04:13,775 --> 01:04:15,151
(CAR APPROACHlNG)

540
01:04:31,877 --> 01:04:33,753
- Evening.
- Evening.

541
01:04:33,837 --> 01:04:35,588
I almost drove right past.

542
01:04:35,672 --> 01:04:38,716
I'm always forgetting to turn the sign on,
but we do have a vacancy.

543
01:04:38,800 --> 01:04:41,802
Twelve, in fact.
Twelve cabins, twelve vacancies. Candy?

544
01:04:41,887 --> 01:04:43,429
No, thanks.

545
01:04:43,513 --> 01:04:46,557
Last two days, I've been to so many
motels my eyes are bleary with neon,

546
01:04:46,641 --> 01:04:50,186
but, you know, this is the first place
that looks like it's hiding from the world.

547
01:04:50,270 --> 01:04:53,147
Well, I'll tell you the truth.
I didn't really forget to turn the sign on.

548
01:04:53,231 --> 01:04:56,484
Just doesn't seem like any use any more, you know.

549
01:04:57,486 --> 01:05:00,363
See, that used to be the main highway right there.

550
01:05:00,447 --> 01:05:01,697
Well, wanna come in and register?

551
01:05:01,782 --> 01:05:03,240
No, no, sit down. I don't wanna trouble you.

552
01:05:03,325 --> 01:05:06,535
- I just wanna ask a few questions.
- No, that's no trouble. Today's linen day.

553
01:05:06,620 --> 01:05:09,205
I always change the beds here once a week
whether they've been used or not.

554
01:05:09,289 --> 01:05:11,707
Hate the smell of dampness, don't you?
It's such a...

555
01:05:11,792 --> 01:05:14,543
I don't know, creepy smell. Come on.

556
01:05:15,837 --> 01:05:17,797
You out to buy a motel?

557
01:05:19,466 --> 01:05:20,633
No.

558
01:05:21,134 --> 01:05:22,635
Reason I ask, you said you'd seen so many

559
01:05:22,719 --> 01:05:25,888
in the past couple of days,
I thought maybe you...

560
01:05:25,972 --> 01:05:28,599
What... What was it you wanted to ask?

561
01:05:28,683 --> 01:05:31,477
Well, you see, I'm looking for a missing person.

562
01:05:31,561 --> 01:05:34,814
My name's Arbogast.
I'm a private investigator.

563
01:05:34,898 --> 01:05:36,732
I've been trying to trace a girl

564
01:05:36,817 --> 01:05:40,569
that's been missing for, oh,
about a week now from Phoenix.

565
01:05:40,654 --> 01:05:43,197
It's a private matter.
The family wants to forgive her.

566
01:05:43,281 --> 01:05:45,408
She's not in any trouble.

567
01:05:45,867 --> 01:05:48,661
I didn't think the police went looking
for people who aren't in trouble.

568
01:05:48,745 --> 01:05:50,871
- I'm not the police.
- Oh, yeah.

569
01:05:50,956 --> 01:05:52,790
We have reason to believe
that she came along this way.

570
01:05:52,874 --> 01:05:55,334
May have stopped in the area.
Did she stop here?

571
01:05:55,419 --> 01:05:58,003
Well, no one's stopped here for a couple of weeks.

572
01:05:58,088 --> 01:06:00,423
Would you mind looking at the picture
before committing yourself?

573
01:06:00,507 --> 01:06:01,757
Commit myself?

574
01:06:01,842 --> 01:06:05,719
- You sure talk like a policeman.
- Look at the picture, please.

575
01:06:06,388 --> 01:06:07,430
Mmm-mmm.

576
01:06:07,514 --> 01:06:09,223
- Sure?
- Yeah.

577
01:06:10,142 --> 01:06:12,435
Well, she may have used an alias.

578
01:06:12,519 --> 01:06:13,978
Marion Crane's her real name,

579
01:06:14,062 --> 01:06:17,314
but she could've registered under a different one.

580
01:06:17,858 --> 01:06:21,902
Well, I tell you, I don't even much bother
with guests registering any more.

581
01:06:21,987 --> 01:06:24,655
You know, one by one, you drop the formalities.

582
01:06:24,739 --> 01:06:27,116
I shouldn't even bother changing the sheets,

583
01:06:27,200 --> 01:06:29,452
but old habits die hard.

584
01:06:29,465 --> 01:06:31,328
Which reminds me...

585
01:06:32,330 --> 01:06:35,207
- What's that?
- It's the light. The sign.

586
01:06:35,667 --> 01:06:38,461
We had a couple last week,
said if the thing hadn't been on,

587
01:06:38,545 --> 01:06:40,629
they would've thought
this was an old, deserted...

588
01:06:40,714 --> 01:06:43,007
Well, you see, that's exactly my point.

589
01:06:43,091 --> 01:06:44,967
You said that nobody'd been here
for a couple weeks,

590
01:06:45,051 --> 01:06:47,845
and there's a couple came by
and didn't know that you were open.

591
01:06:47,929 --> 01:06:48,929
Yeah.

592
01:06:49,431 --> 01:06:51,307
Well, as you say, old habits die hard.

593
01:06:51,391 --> 01:06:54,310
It's possible, this girl could've registered
under another name.

594
01:06:54,394 --> 01:06:56,020
Do you mind if I look at your book?

595
01:06:56,104 --> 01:06:58,063
- No.
- Thank you.

596
01:07:01,151 --> 01:07:02,318
All right.

597
01:07:02,402 --> 01:07:04,653
I'll get the date somewhere.

598
01:07:06,114 --> 01:07:07,740
See, there's nobody.

599
01:07:07,824 --> 01:07:11,577
Let's see now, I have a sample of her
handwriting here.

600
01:07:13,413 --> 01:07:14,705
Oh, yes.

601
01:07:15,624 --> 01:07:17,041
Here we are.

602
01:07:17,375 --> 01:07:20,336
Marie Samuels. That's an interesting alias.

603
01:07:21,379 --> 01:07:23,797
- Is that her?
- Yeah. Yeah, I think so.

604
01:07:23,882 --> 01:07:25,883
Marie, Marion.

605
01:07:25,967 --> 01:07:28,636
Samuels. Her boyfriend's name is Sam.

606
01:07:28,720 --> 01:07:29,720
Mmm-hmm.

607
01:07:30,138 --> 01:07:32,640
Was she in disguise, by any chance?

608
01:07:34,184 --> 01:07:37,561
- Wanna check the picture again?
- Look, I wasn't lying to you, Mr...

609
01:07:37,646 --> 01:07:39,688
Oh, I know that. I know you wouldn't lie.

610
01:07:39,773 --> 01:07:41,649
You know, it's tough keeping track
of the time around here.

611
01:07:41,733 --> 01:07:43,484
Oh, I know, I know.

612
01:07:44,277 --> 01:07:45,861
Oh, yeah.

613
01:07:45,946 --> 01:07:48,113
Well, it was raining

614
01:07:49,407 --> 01:07:51,158
and her hair was all wet.

615
01:07:51,243 --> 01:07:53,953
I tell you, it's not really
a very good picture of her either.

616
01:07:54,037 --> 01:07:55,663
No, I guess not.

617
01:07:55,747 --> 01:07:57,248
Tell me all about her.

618
01:07:57,332 --> 01:07:58,624
Well,

619
01:07:59,084 --> 01:08:03,337
she arrived rather late one night,
and she went straight to sleep

620
01:08:03,421 --> 01:08:06,048
and left early the next morning.

621
01:08:06,132 --> 01:08:09,260
- How early?
- Oh, very early.

622
01:08:09,344 --> 01:08:12,555
Mmm-hmm. Which morning was that?

623
01:08:12,639 --> 01:08:13,889
The...

624
01:08:16,810 --> 01:08:19,311
- The next morning. Sunday.
- I see.

625
01:08:20,272 --> 01:08:22,314
- Did anyone meet her here?
- No.

626
01:08:22,399 --> 01:08:25,317
- Did she arrive with anyone?
- No.

627
01:08:25,777 --> 01:08:27,903
- Did she make any phone calls or...
- No.

628
01:08:27,988 --> 01:08:29,321
Locally?

629
01:08:30,240 --> 01:08:32,658
Did you spend the night with her?

630
01:08:32,909 --> 01:08:34,159
No.

631
01:08:34,244 --> 01:08:38,038
Well then, how would you know
that she didn't make any phone calls?

632
01:08:39,457 --> 01:08:40,666
(STAMMERlNG)

633
01:08:41,751 --> 01:08:43,711
Well, she was very tired and...

634
01:08:43,795 --> 01:08:47,089
See, now I'm starting to remember.

635
01:08:47,173 --> 01:08:48,757
I'm making a mental picture of it in my mind.

636
01:08:48,842 --> 01:08:50,718
You know, if you make a mental
picturisation of something...

637
01:08:50,802 --> 01:08:53,429
That's right. That's right. Take your time.

638
01:08:53,513 --> 01:08:55,848
She was... She was sitting back there...

639
01:08:55,932 --> 01:08:58,851
No, no, she was standing back there
with a sandwich in her hand

640
01:08:58,935 --> 01:09:00,603
and she said she had to go to sleep early

641
01:09:00,687 --> 01:09:05,691
because she had a long drive ahead of her.

642
01:09:06,318 --> 01:09:08,277
- Back where?
- Back where she came from.

643
01:09:08,361 --> 01:09:11,155
No, you said before that she was
sitting back there... Standing back there...

644
01:09:11,239 --> 01:09:13,699
Yes, back in my parlour there.

645
01:09:13,783 --> 01:09:15,909
She was very hungry
and I made her a sandwich

646
01:09:15,994 --> 01:09:21,415
and then she said that she was tired
and she had to go right to bed.

647
01:09:21,499 --> 01:09:23,667
I see. How did she pay you?

648
01:09:23,752 --> 01:09:25,085
- Cash? Cheque?
- Cash.

649
01:09:25,170 --> 01:09:26,503
Cash, huh?

650
01:09:27,589 --> 01:09:31,258
- And after she left, she didn't come back?
- Well, why should she?

651
01:09:31,343 --> 01:09:32,343
Yeah.

652
01:09:34,095 --> 01:09:35,387
(CLEARS THROAT)

653
01:09:36,973 --> 01:09:40,059
Well, Mr Arbogast, I guess that's about it.

654
01:09:40,810 --> 01:09:43,395
I've got some work to do, if you don't mind.

655
01:09:43,480 --> 01:09:45,481
Well, to tell you the truth, I do mind.

656
01:09:45,565 --> 01:09:49,693
You see, if it doesn't jell, it isn't aspic,
and this ain't jelling.

657
01:09:49,778 --> 01:09:52,863
It's not coming together. Something's missing.

658
01:09:53,323 --> 01:09:56,784
Well, I don't know
what you could expect me to know.

659
01:09:56,868 --> 01:09:58,327
People just come and go, you know.

660
01:09:58,411 --> 01:10:02,039
- That's right. She isn't still here, is she?
- No.

661
01:10:04,084 --> 01:10:07,044
If I wanted to check the cabins, all 12 of them,

662
01:10:07,128 --> 01:10:08,379
I'd need a warrant, wouldn't I?

663
01:10:08,463 --> 01:10:11,340
Listen, if you don't believe me, come on
with me. You can help me change beds.

664
01:10:11,424 --> 01:10:14,009
- Okay?
- No, thanks.

665
01:10:31,778 --> 01:10:33,445
Change your mind?

666
01:10:36,700 --> 01:10:40,786
You know, I... I think I must have one
of those faces you just can't help believing.

667
01:10:40,870 --> 01:10:42,663
- Is anyone at home?
- No.

668
01:10:42,747 --> 01:10:45,040
Oh? There's somebody sitting up in the window.

669
01:10:45,125 --> 01:10:47,501
- No, no, there isn't.
- Sure, go ahead. Take a look.

670
01:10:47,585 --> 01:10:49,545
Oh, that must be my mother.

671
01:10:49,629 --> 01:10:52,089
She's an invalid.

672
01:10:52,173 --> 01:10:54,174
It's practically like living alone.

673
01:10:54,259 --> 01:10:55,759
Oh, I see.

674
01:10:56,970 --> 01:11:01,390
Now, if this girl, Marion Crane, were here,
you wouldn't be hiding her, would you?

675
01:11:01,474 --> 01:11:03,809
- No.
- Not even if she paid you well?

676
01:11:03,893 --> 01:11:04,977
No.

677
01:11:05,645 --> 01:11:10,733
Let's just say, just for the sake of argument,
that she wanted you to gallantly protect her.

678
01:11:10,817 --> 01:11:12,693
You'd know that you were being used.

679
01:11:12,777 --> 01:11:14,069
You wouldn't be made a fool of, would you?

680
01:11:14,154 --> 01:11:15,738
- But I'm not a fool.
- Well, I...

681
01:11:15,822 --> 01:11:18,866
And I'm not capable of being fooled,
not even by a woman.

682
01:11:18,950 --> 01:11:21,368
- Well, this is not a slur on your manhood.
- Let's put it this way.

683
01:11:21,453 --> 01:11:25,330
She might have fooled me,
but she didn't fool my mother.

684
01:11:25,415 --> 01:11:27,332
Well, then your mother met her.
Could I talk to your mother?

685
01:11:27,417 --> 01:11:30,085
No. As I told you, she's confined.

686
01:11:30,170 --> 01:11:31,420
Yes, but just for a few minutes. That's all.

687
01:11:31,504 --> 01:11:33,589
There might be some hint that you missed out on.

688
01:11:33,673 --> 01:11:36,008
You know, sick old women are usually pretty sharp.

689
01:11:36,092 --> 01:11:39,762
- Mr Arbogast... I think I've...
- Just for a moment. I wouldn't disturb her.

690
01:11:39,846 --> 01:11:42,681
- I think I've talked to you all I want to.
- Yes, but just for...

691
01:11:42,766 --> 01:11:45,058
I think it'd be much better if you left now. Thanks.

692
01:11:46,019 --> 01:11:47,895
Well, all right.

693
01:11:48,646 --> 01:11:51,940
You sure would save me a lot of leg work
if you'd let me talk to her.

694
01:11:52,025 --> 01:11:53,442
- Would I need a warrant for that, too?
- Sure.

695
01:11:53,526 --> 01:11:54,526
Uh-huh.

696
01:11:55,487 --> 01:11:57,696
All right. Thanks anyway.

697
01:12:08,208 --> 01:12:09,500
(STARTS ENGlNE)

698
01:12:15,840 --> 01:12:17,424
(CAR PULLS AWAY)

699
01:12:51,876 --> 01:12:54,962
Hello, Loomis. This is Arbogast.
Is Lila there?

700
01:12:55,338 --> 01:12:57,506
Okay. Let me talk to her, please.

701
01:12:58,842 --> 01:13:00,300
Hello, Lila.

702
01:13:00,969 --> 01:13:03,762
Lila, listen. Marion was up here.

703
01:13:04,764 --> 01:13:07,474
Yes, she spent last Saturday night at the Bates Motel.

704
01:13:07,559 --> 01:13:09,351
It's right out here on the old highway.

705
01:13:09,435 --> 01:13:12,896
I even know what cabin she was in.
It was number one.

706
01:13:13,398 --> 01:13:17,693
Well, this young fella that runs the place
said that she just spent the night,

707
01:13:17,777 --> 01:13:20,279
left the next day and that was it.

708
01:13:22,782 --> 01:13:24,449
No, not exactly.

709
01:13:25,618 --> 01:13:30,038
Well, I did question him, believe me,
but I think I got all there was to get.

710
01:13:30,123 --> 01:13:33,292
I'll just have to pick up the pieces from here.

711
01:13:33,751 --> 01:13:37,337
Well, I tell you, I don't feel entirely satisfied...

712
01:13:37,422 --> 01:13:39,756
See, this boy had a sick old mother.

713
01:13:39,841 --> 01:13:42,759
I think she saw Marion and talked to her.

714
01:13:42,844 --> 01:13:46,096
No. No, unfortunately, he wouldn't let me see her.

715
01:13:46,514 --> 01:13:50,309
Well, I was, but I think I'll go back to the motel first.

716
01:13:51,311 --> 01:13:54,855
No, you stay there with Loomis.
I'll be back in about an hour.

717
01:13:54,939 --> 01:13:57,024
All right, fine. Listen, I...

718
01:13:57,108 --> 01:13:59,568
You'll be happy to know what I think.

719
01:13:59,652 --> 01:14:03,655
I think our friend Sam Loomis
didn't know that Marion was here.

720
01:14:05,366 --> 01:14:08,535
Yeah. All right.
See you in about an hour or less.

721
01:14:08,620 --> 01:14:09,661
All right, bye-bye.

722
01:15:06,344 --> 01:15:07,552
Bates?

723
01:17:36,786 --> 01:17:38,245
(ARBOGAST SCREAMS)

724
01:17:41,374 --> 01:17:43,959
Sometimes Saturday night has a lonely sound.

725
01:17:44,043 --> 01:17:46,128
Ever notice that, Lila?

726
01:17:47,547 --> 01:17:49,840
Sam, he said an hour or less.

727
01:17:50,424 --> 01:17:51,716
SAM: Yeah.

728
01:17:53,136 --> 01:17:57,013
- (SlGHS) It's been three.
- Well, are we just going to sit here and wait?

729
01:17:57,098 --> 01:17:58,682
He'll be back.

730
01:17:59,058 --> 01:18:01,560
Let's sit still and hang on, okay?

731
01:18:06,899 --> 01:18:10,318
- How far is the old highway?
- You wanna go out there, don't you?

732
01:18:10,403 --> 01:18:11,903
- Bust in on Arbogast and the old lady...
- Yes.

733
01:18:11,988 --> 01:18:13,655
- Maybe shake her up.
- Yes!

734
01:18:13,739 --> 01:18:17,159
- That wouldn't be a wise thing to do.
- Patience doesn't run in my family, Sam.

735
01:18:17,243 --> 01:18:18,869
- I'm going out there.
- Look, Arbogast said...

736
01:18:18,953 --> 01:18:20,871
An hour, or less.

737
01:18:25,585 --> 01:18:28,753
- Well, I'm going.
- You'll never find it.

738
01:18:32,717 --> 01:18:35,010
- Stay here.
- Why can't I go with you?

739
01:18:35,094 --> 01:18:38,889
I don't know. One of us has to be here,
in case he's on the way.

740
01:18:38,973 --> 01:18:41,141
What am I supposed to do, just sit here and wait?

741
01:18:41,225 --> 01:18:43,059
Yeah. Stay here.

742
01:19:03,789 --> 01:19:05,081
Arbogast?

743
01:19:06,459 --> 01:19:07,792
Arbogast!

744
01:19:11,088 --> 01:19:12,547
(ECHOlNG) Arbogast!

745
01:19:29,357 --> 01:19:31,191
(CAR APPROACHES)

746
01:19:31,359 --> 01:19:32,484
(CAR STOPS)

747
01:19:33,527 --> 01:19:34,819
(DOOR OPENlNG)

748
01:19:42,662 --> 01:19:45,080
- He didn't come back here?
- Sam!

749
01:19:45,164 --> 01:19:48,708
No Arbogast, no Bates.
Only the old lady at home.

750
01:19:48,793 --> 01:19:51,753
Sick old lady unable to answer the door, or unwilling.

751
01:19:51,837 --> 01:19:53,755
Where could he have gone?

752
01:19:53,839 --> 01:19:56,007
Maybe he got some definite lead.
Maybe he went right on.

753
01:19:56,133 --> 01:19:58,385
- Without calling me?
- In a hurry.

754
01:19:58,469 --> 01:20:01,513
Sam, he called when he had nothing.
Nothing but a dissatisfied feeling.

755
01:20:01,597 --> 01:20:05,308
Don't you think he'd have called
if he had anything at all?

756
01:20:05,935 --> 01:20:07,936
Yes, I think he would've.

757
01:20:08,020 --> 01:20:10,146
- Let's go see Al Chambers.
- Who's he?

758
01:20:10,231 --> 01:20:13,942
- Our deputy sheriff around here.
- All right. Let me get my coat.

759
01:20:21,993 --> 01:20:23,451
SAM: Good evening.

760
01:20:24,078 --> 01:20:27,998
Well, I don't know where to start,
except at the beginning.

761
01:20:28,082 --> 01:20:29,207
Yes.

762
01:20:30,334 --> 01:20:33,211
- This is Lila Crane from Phoenix.
- How do you do?

763
01:20:33,296 --> 01:20:38,258
She's been here searching for her sister.
There's this private detective helping and...

764
01:20:38,342 --> 01:20:40,385
Well, we got a call from this detective

765
01:20:40,469 --> 01:20:42,846
saying that he'd traced her to that motel
out on the old highway.

766
01:20:42,930 --> 01:20:44,889
That must be the Bates Motel.

767
01:20:44,974 --> 01:20:47,892
He traced her there and called us to say
he was going to question Mrs Bates.

768
01:20:47,977 --> 01:20:49,561
Norman took a wife?

769
01:20:49,645 --> 01:20:52,981
SAM: No, I don't think so.
An old woman. His mother.

770
01:20:53,441 --> 01:20:56,484
Well, anyway, that was early this evening.
We haven't seen or heard from him since.

771
01:20:56,569 --> 01:21:00,071
Now, your sister's missing how long?

772
01:21:00,156 --> 01:21:03,325
Well, she left Phoenix a week ago yesterday,
without a trace.

773
01:21:03,409 --> 01:21:06,911
How'd you and this detective
come to trace her to Fairvale?

774
01:21:06,996 --> 01:21:09,164
They thought she'd be coming to me.

775
01:21:09,248 --> 01:21:11,583
- Left Phoenix under her own steam?
- Yes.

776
01:21:11,667 --> 01:21:14,252
She's not missing so much as she's run away.

777
01:21:14,337 --> 01:21:16,588
- SAM: That's right.
- From what?

778
01:21:19,592 --> 01:21:22,135
- She stole some money.
- SHERlFF: A lot?

779
01:21:24,180 --> 01:21:25,513
$40,000.

780
01:21:26,098 --> 01:21:27,682
And the police haven't been able to...

781
01:21:27,767 --> 01:21:31,603
Everyone concerned thought if they
could get her to give the money back,

782
01:21:31,687 --> 01:21:34,147
they could avoid involving her with the police.

783
01:21:34,231 --> 01:21:38,151
That explains the private detective.
He traced her to the Bates place.

784
01:21:38,235 --> 01:21:40,695
What exactly did he say when he called you?

785
01:21:40,780 --> 01:21:44,574
Well, he said that Marion was there
for one night and then she left.

786
01:21:44,658 --> 01:21:46,368
With the $40,000?

787
01:21:47,078 --> 01:21:49,204
Well, he didn't say anything about the money.

788
01:21:49,288 --> 01:21:51,706
It isn't important what he said on the phone, is it?

789
01:21:51,791 --> 01:21:53,375
He was supposed to come back here and talk to us

790
01:21:53,459 --> 01:21:55,543
after he talked to the mother, and he didn't.

791
01:21:55,628 --> 01:21:57,837
That's what I want you to do something about.

792
01:21:57,922 --> 01:21:59,422
Like what?

793
01:22:00,299 --> 01:22:01,925
I'm sorry if I seem overanxious.

794
01:22:02,009 --> 01:22:05,720
It's just that I'm sure there's something
wrong out there and I have to know what.

795
01:22:05,805 --> 01:22:09,265
Well, I think there's something wrong
too, miss, but not the same thing.

796
01:22:09,350 --> 01:22:12,519
I think what's wrong is your private detective.

797
01:22:12,603 --> 01:22:16,564
I think he got himself a hot lead
as to where your sister was going,

798
01:22:16,649 --> 01:22:18,733
probably from Norman Bates,

799
01:22:18,818 --> 01:22:23,029
and called you to keep you still
while he took off after her and the money.

800
01:22:23,114 --> 01:22:26,950
No, no, he said he was dissatisfied
and he was going back there.

801
01:22:27,034 --> 01:22:29,411
Why don't you call Norman
and let him say just what happened?

802
01:22:29,495 --> 01:22:30,870
SHERlFF: At this hour?

803
01:22:30,955 --> 01:22:33,164
Well, he was out
when I was there just a while ago.

804
01:22:33,249 --> 01:22:35,291
If he's back, he probably isn't even in bed yet.

805
01:22:35,376 --> 01:22:36,918
He wasn't out when you were there,

806
01:22:37,002 --> 01:22:40,338
he just wasn't answering the door
in the dead of night, like some people do.

807
01:22:40,423 --> 01:22:42,674
This fellow lives like a hermit.

808
01:22:42,758 --> 01:22:45,885
You must remember that bad business
out there about 10 years ago.

809
01:22:45,970 --> 01:22:47,595
Please. Call.

810
01:22:53,853 --> 01:22:58,022
Florrie, the Sheriff wants you to
connect him with the Bates Motel.

811
01:23:04,113 --> 01:23:06,698
Hello, Norman? Sheriff Chambers.

812
01:23:08,367 --> 01:23:10,118
I've been just fine, thanks.

813
01:23:10,202 --> 01:23:12,370
Listen, we got worries here.

814
01:23:12,955 --> 01:23:16,374
Yeah, have you had a fella stop by there tonight...

815
01:23:17,543 --> 01:23:19,836
Well, this one wouldn't be a customer, anyway.

816
01:23:19,920 --> 01:23:22,881
- He's a private detective, name of...
- Arbogast.

817
01:23:22,965 --> 01:23:24,549
Arbogast.

818
01:23:28,095 --> 01:23:30,054
And after he left?

819
01:23:32,391 --> 01:23:33,933
No, that's okay, Norman.

820
01:23:35,019 --> 01:23:36,186
(HANGS UP PHONE)

821
01:23:36,270 --> 01:23:39,647
This detective was there,
Norman told him about the girl,

822
01:23:39,732 --> 01:23:42,609
the detective thanked him and he went away.

823
01:23:43,360 --> 01:23:46,613
And he didn't come back?
He didn't see the mother?

824
01:23:46,697 --> 01:23:48,948
Your detective told you
he couldn't come right back

825
01:23:49,033 --> 01:23:52,911
because he was going to question
Norman Bates' mother, right?

826
01:23:52,995 --> 01:23:54,120
Yes.

827
01:23:54,747 --> 01:23:57,540
Norman Bates' mother has been dead and buried

828
01:23:57,625 --> 01:24:00,710
in Green Lawn Cemetery for the past 10 years.

829
01:24:02,213 --> 01:24:05,924
I helped Norman pick out the dress
she was buried in. Periwinkle blue.

830
01:24:06,008 --> 01:24:07,675
SHERlFF: It ain't only local history, Sam.

831
01:24:07,760 --> 01:24:11,554
It's the only case of murder and suicide
on Fairvale ledgers.

832
01:24:11,639 --> 01:24:16,100
Mrs Bates poisoned this guy she was involved with

833
01:24:16,185 --> 01:24:18,436
when she found out he was married.

834
01:24:18,521 --> 01:24:21,606
Then took a helping of the same stuff herself.

835
01:24:21,690 --> 01:24:24,609
Strychnine. Ugly way to die.

836
01:24:25,611 --> 01:24:27,862
Norman found them dead together.

837
01:24:27,947 --> 01:24:29,155
(WHlSPERlNG) In bed.

838
01:24:30,741 --> 01:24:33,785
You mean that old woman I saw sitting in
the window out there wasn't Bates' mother?

839
01:24:33,869 --> 01:24:37,914
Now, wait a minute, Sam.
Are you sure you saw an old woman?

840
01:24:37,998 --> 01:24:40,458
Yes! In the house behind the motel!

841
01:24:40,543 --> 01:24:42,835
I called and pounded but she just ignored me!

842
01:24:42,920 --> 01:24:45,672
You want to tell me
you saw Norman Bates' mother?

843
01:24:45,756 --> 01:24:48,967
But it had to be because Arbogast said so, too!

844
01:24:49,593 --> 01:24:52,720
And the young man wouldn't let him
see her because she was too ill.

845
01:24:52,805 --> 01:24:55,682
Well, if the woman up there is Mrs Bates,

846
01:24:57,017 --> 01:25:00,311
who's that woman buried out in Green Lawn Cemetery?

847
01:25:43,689 --> 01:25:47,066
NORMAN: Now, Mother, I'm gonna bring something up...

848
01:25:47,192 --> 01:25:48,651
(MRS. BATES LAUGHS)

849
01:25:48,736 --> 01:25:49,986
MRS. BATES: I am sorry, boy,

850
01:25:50,070 --> 01:25:53,740
but you do manage to look ludicrous
when you give me orders.

851
01:25:53,824 --> 01:25:55,450
NORMAN: Please, Mother.

852
01:25:55,534 --> 01:25:58,453
MRS. BATES: No! I will not hide in the fruit cellar.

853
01:25:58,746 --> 01:25:59,787
(MRS. BATES LAUGHS)

854
01:25:59,872 --> 01:26:01,998
You think I'm fruity, huh?

855
01:26:02,082 --> 01:26:04,167
I'm staying right here.

856
01:26:04,251 --> 01:26:07,086
This is my room
and no one will drag me out of it,

857
01:26:07,212 --> 01:26:10,298
least of all my big, bold son.

858
01:26:10,382 --> 01:26:11,466
NORMAN: They'll come now, Mother.

859
01:26:11,550 --> 01:26:15,470
He came after the girl,
and now someone will come after him.

860
01:26:15,554 --> 01:26:17,305
Mother, please, it's just for a few days.

861
01:26:17,389 --> 01:26:19,807
Just for a few days so they won't find you.

862
01:26:19,892 --> 01:26:21,684
MRS. BATES: Just for a few days?

863
01:26:22,186 --> 01:26:25,438
In that dark, dank fruit cellar? No!

864
01:26:26,065 --> 01:26:29,442
You hid me there once, boy,
and you won't do it again, not ever again!

865
01:26:29,526 --> 01:26:31,069
Now get out!

866
01:26:32,613 --> 01:26:36,074
- I told you to get out, boy.
- NORMAN: I'll carry you, Mother.

867
01:26:36,450 --> 01:26:39,369
MRS. BATES: Norman,
what do you think you're doing?

868
01:26:39,453 --> 01:26:42,747
Don't you touch me! Don't! Norman!

869
01:26:48,212 --> 01:26:51,381
Put me down! Put me down!
I can walk on my own.

870
01:26:52,132 --> 01:26:53,758
(CHURCH BELL RlNGlNG)

871
01:26:57,721 --> 01:26:59,681
(lNDlSTlNCT CHATTER)

872
01:26:59,765 --> 01:27:00,973
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

873
01:27:01,058 --> 01:27:02,517
Good morning.

874
01:27:02,601 --> 01:27:04,894
We thought if you didn't mind,
we'd drive out to that motel with you.

875
01:27:04,978 --> 01:27:07,271
- He's already been.
- Went out before service.

876
01:27:07,356 --> 01:27:09,857
- Did you two have breakfast?
- You didn't find anything?

877
01:27:09,942 --> 01:27:12,902
Nothing. Let's clear the way here.

878
01:27:13,737 --> 01:27:16,114
Well, what did he say about my sister?

879
01:27:16,198 --> 01:27:19,367
Just what he told your detective,
she used a fake name.

880
01:27:19,451 --> 01:27:20,702
Saw the register myself.

881
01:27:20,786 --> 01:27:23,705
Saw the whole place, as a matter of fact.
That boy's alone there.

882
01:27:23,789 --> 01:27:26,791
- No mother?
- You must have seen an illusion, Sam.

883
01:27:26,875 --> 01:27:29,752
I know you're not the seeing-illusions type,

884
01:27:29,837 --> 01:27:32,338
but no woman was there
and I don't believe in ghosts,

885
01:27:32,423 --> 01:27:34,257
- so there it is.
- I still feel that there's something...

886
01:27:34,341 --> 01:27:37,969
Can see you do.
I'm sorry I couldn't make you feel better.

887
01:27:38,053 --> 01:27:39,762
You want to come around
to my office this afternoon

888
01:27:39,847 --> 01:27:42,932
and report a missing person and a theft,
that's what you want to do.

889
01:27:43,016 --> 01:27:45,059
The sooner you drop this in the lap of the law,

890
01:27:45,144 --> 01:27:48,980
that's the sooner you stand a chance
of your sister being picked up.

891
01:27:49,064 --> 01:27:50,648
How about that?

892
01:27:51,191 --> 01:27:53,609
- I don't know.
- It's Sunday.

893
01:27:54,361 --> 01:27:56,863
Come on over to the house
and do your reporting around dinnertime.

894
01:27:56,947 --> 01:27:58,740
It'll make it nicer.

895
01:27:59,324 --> 01:28:01,659
- You too, Sam.
- Thank you.

896
01:28:04,037 --> 01:28:07,874
- Maybe I am the seeing-illusions type.
- No, you're not.

897
01:28:09,001 --> 01:28:10,752
Well, you want me to drop you at the hotel or...

898
01:28:10,836 --> 01:28:11,878
Sam.

899
01:28:11,962 --> 01:28:15,298
I still won't feel satisfied until I go out there.

900
01:28:16,508 --> 01:28:18,593
Neither will I. Come on.

901
01:28:19,845 --> 01:28:24,056
We better decide what we're gonna say
or do when we walk in there.

902
01:28:24,141 --> 01:28:26,434
We're going to register as man and wife.

903
01:28:26,518 --> 01:28:29,103
We're going to get shown to a cabin,

904
01:28:29,605 --> 01:28:33,524
and then we're going to search every
inch of the place, inside and out.

905
01:28:40,449 --> 01:28:42,033
(CAR DOOR OPENS)

906
01:28:57,424 --> 01:28:59,217
I wonder where Norman Bates does his hermiting.

907
01:28:59,301 --> 01:29:01,803
Someone's at that window.
I just saw the curtain move.

908
01:29:01,887 --> 01:29:03,179
Come on.

909
01:29:05,933 --> 01:29:08,434
- Well?
- Just coming up to ring for you.

910
01:29:08,519 --> 01:29:09,519
Uh-huh.

911
01:29:09,728 --> 01:29:11,062
Suppose you want a room.

912
01:29:11,146 --> 01:29:12,897
We were gonna try to make it
straight to San Francisco,

913
01:29:12,981 --> 01:29:15,107
but we don't like the look of that sky.

914
01:29:15,192 --> 01:29:18,361
- Looks like a bad day coming, doesn't it?
- Okay.

915
01:29:25,202 --> 01:29:27,745
- I'll take you to Cabin 10.
- Better sign in first, hadn't we?

916
01:29:27,830 --> 01:29:29,705
No. That's not necessary.

917
01:29:29,790 --> 01:29:33,835
My boss is paying for this trip
and, well, it's 90% business.

918
01:29:33,919 --> 01:29:36,671
He wants practically notarised receipts.

919
01:29:36,755 --> 01:29:39,382
I'd better sign in and get a receipt.

920
01:29:57,109 --> 01:29:58,484
Thank you.

921
01:30:02,322 --> 01:30:04,782
- I'll get your bags.
- Haven't any.

922
01:30:07,286 --> 01:30:10,454
- I'll show you the room, then.
- SAM: First time I've ever seen it happen.

923
01:30:10,539 --> 01:30:12,498
You check in any other place
in this country without bags

924
01:30:12,583 --> 01:30:14,625
and you have to pay in advance.

925
01:30:14,710 --> 01:30:15,960
$10.

926
01:30:20,799 --> 01:30:22,174
(PAPER CRlNKLlNG)

927
01:30:23,468 --> 01:30:25,011
SAM: That receipt.

928
01:30:30,976 --> 01:30:32,685
I'll go on ahead.

929
01:30:38,317 --> 01:30:41,402
NORMAN: All right, there's your receipt.
Now, I'll show you the cabin.

930
01:30:41,486 --> 01:30:44,196
Don't bother yourself. We'll find it.

931
01:31:02,799 --> 01:31:04,592
Sam, we have to go into that cabin and search it,

932
01:31:04,676 --> 01:31:08,179
no matter what we're afraid of finding
or how much it may hurt.

933
01:31:08,263 --> 01:31:09,513
I know.

934
01:31:10,057 --> 01:31:11,807
Do you think if something happened,
it happened there?

935
01:31:11,892 --> 01:31:13,392
I don't know.

936
01:31:13,727 --> 01:31:16,228
But if you had a useless business like this motel,

937
01:31:16,313 --> 01:31:17,480
what would you need to get out?

938
01:31:17,564 --> 01:31:19,607
To get a new business somewhere else?

939
01:31:19,691 --> 01:31:21,067
$40,000?

940
01:31:23,779 --> 01:31:25,905
How could we prove that...

941
01:31:25,989 --> 01:31:28,574
Well, if he opens a motel on
the new highway in, say, a year...

942
01:31:28,659 --> 01:31:31,160
There must be some proof that exists now,

943
01:31:31,244 --> 01:31:34,789
something that proves he got that money
away from Marion somehow.

944
01:31:34,873 --> 01:31:39,043
- What makes you sound so certain?
- Arbogast. He liked me, Sam.

945
01:31:39,544 --> 01:31:43,047
Or he felt sorry for me and he was
beginning to feel the same way about you.

946
01:31:43,131 --> 01:31:45,841
I could tell the last time
I talked to him on the phone.

947
01:31:45,926 --> 01:31:47,259
He wouldn't have gone anywhere

948
01:31:47,344 --> 01:31:50,054
or done anything without telling us,
unless he was stopped.

949
01:31:50,138 --> 01:31:53,975
And he was stopped.
So he must have found out something.

950
01:31:56,186 --> 01:31:58,354
We'll start with Cabin One.

951
01:32:00,732 --> 01:32:03,734
If he sees us, we're just taking the air.

952
01:32:35,726 --> 01:32:36,934
SAM: Bates.

953
01:33:27,319 --> 01:33:29,612
- No shower curtain.
- Sam!

954
01:33:29,696 --> 01:33:30,821
- Look!
- What is it?

955
01:33:30,906 --> 01:33:33,240
It's figuring. It didn't get washed down. Look.

956
01:33:33,325 --> 01:33:37,161
Some figure has been added to
or subtracted from 40,000.

957
01:33:37,245 --> 01:33:38,454
That proves Marion was here.

958
01:33:38,538 --> 01:33:41,832
- It'd be too wild a coincidence...
- Bates never denied she was here.

959
01:33:42,959 --> 01:33:44,919
Well, doesn't that prove that he found out
about the money?

960
01:33:45,003 --> 01:33:46,712
Do we simply ask him where he's hidden it?

961
01:33:46,797 --> 01:33:47,797
No.

962
01:33:47,881 --> 01:33:51,383
But that old woman, whoever she is,
she told Arbogast something.

963
01:33:51,468 --> 01:33:54,136
I want her to tell us the same thing.

964
01:33:54,596 --> 01:33:57,264
- You can't go up there.
- Why not?

965
01:33:57,349 --> 01:33:59,266
- Bates.
- Well, let's find him.

966
01:33:59,351 --> 01:34:02,061
One of us can keep him occupied
while the other gets to the old woman.

967
01:34:02,145 --> 01:34:05,189
You'll never be able to hold him still
if he doesn't want to be held.

968
01:34:05,273 --> 01:34:10,194
- I don't like you going into that house alone.
- I can handle a sick old woman.

969
01:34:10,529 --> 01:34:13,864
All right, I'll find Bates and keep him occupied.

970
01:34:14,407 --> 01:34:15,950
Wait a minute.

971
01:34:16,535 --> 01:34:19,161
If you get anything out of the mother,

972
01:34:19,871 --> 01:34:22,039
can you find your way back to town?

973
01:34:22,124 --> 01:34:23,707
Yes, of course.

974
01:34:24,459 --> 01:34:27,670
If you do get anything, don't stop to tell me.

975
01:34:50,735 --> 01:34:53,779
- You looking for me?
- Why, yes, as a matter of fact.

976
01:34:53,864 --> 01:34:55,489
Wife's taking a nap

977
01:34:55,574 --> 01:34:59,493
and I never can keep quiet enough for her,
so I just thought I'd look you up and talk.

978
01:34:59,578 --> 01:35:02,955
- Good. You satisfied with your cabin?
- Oh, it's fine.

979
01:36:17,656 --> 01:36:20,241
I've been doing all the talking so far, haven't l?

980
01:36:20,325 --> 01:36:22,409
I thought it was the people
who were alone most of the time

981
01:36:22,494 --> 01:36:24,745
who did all the talking when they got the chance.

982
01:36:24,829 --> 01:36:26,997
Here you are doing all the listening.

983
01:36:27,082 --> 01:36:29,416
- You are alone here, aren't you?
- Mmm-hmm.

984
01:36:29,501 --> 01:36:31,085
Drive me crazy.

985
01:36:32,420 --> 01:36:35,130
I think that would be a rather
extreme reaction, don't you?

986
01:36:35,215 --> 01:36:36,757
Just an expression.

987
01:36:36,841 --> 01:36:40,344
What I meant was
I'd do just about anything to get away.

988
01:36:40,428 --> 01:36:42,179
- Wouldn't you?
- No.

989
01:36:46,643 --> 01:36:48,269
(KNOCKlNG ON DOOR)

990
01:36:48,353 --> 01:36:49,728
LlLA: Mrs Bates?

991
01:37:44,951 --> 01:37:46,160
(GASPS)

992
01:37:46,578 --> 01:37:48,746
(SlGHS)

993
01:38:06,723 --> 01:38:09,099
I'm not saying you shouldn't be contented here,

994
01:38:09,184 --> 01:38:11,060
I'm just doubting that you are.

995
01:38:11,144 --> 01:38:14,813
I think if you saw a chance to get out
from under, you'd unload this place.

996
01:38:14,898 --> 01:38:18,233
This place?
This place happens to be my only world.

997
01:38:19,277 --> 01:38:22,654
I grew up in that house up there.
I had a very happy childhood.

998
01:38:22,739 --> 01:38:24,365
My mother and I were more than happy.

999
01:39:26,344 --> 01:39:28,762
You look frightened.
Have I been saying something frightening?

1000
01:39:28,847 --> 01:39:30,347
I don't know what you've been saying.

1001
01:39:30,432 --> 01:39:33,892
I've been talking about your mother,
about your motel.

1002
01:39:33,977 --> 01:39:36,061
- How you gonna do it?
- Do what?

1003
01:39:36,146 --> 01:39:39,064
Buy a new one in a new town
where you won't have to hide your mother.

1004
01:39:39,149 --> 01:39:41,900
Why don't you just get in your car
and drive away from here, okay?

1005
01:39:41,985 --> 01:39:43,944
Where will you get the money to do that, Bates?

1006
01:39:44,028 --> 01:39:45,946
Or do you already have it socked away?

1007
01:39:46,030 --> 01:39:48,866
- Shut up!
- A lot of it. $40,000.

1008
01:39:52,662 --> 01:39:56,123
I bet your mother knows where
the money is and what you did to get it.

1009
01:39:56,207 --> 01:39:58,208
I think she'll tell us.

1010
01:40:00,044 --> 01:40:03,547
Where's that girl you came here with?
Where is she?

1011
01:41:08,446 --> 01:41:10,030
Mrs Bates...

1012
01:41:20,208 --> 01:41:21,959
(SCREAMlNG)

1013
01:41:23,711 --> 01:41:25,003
(FOOTSTEPS)

1014
01:41:28,007 --> 01:41:29,716
(NORMAN SCREAMlNG)

1015
01:41:30,176 --> 01:41:31,885
I'm Norma Bates!

1016
01:41:31,970 --> 01:41:34,346
(SCREAMlNG)

1017
01:41:57,829 --> 01:41:59,788
(PEOPLE CHATTERlNG)

1018
01:42:03,042 --> 01:42:06,336
If anyone gets any answers
it'll be the psychiatrist.

1019
01:42:06,421 --> 01:42:09,089
Even I couldn't get to Norman and he knows me.

1020
01:42:09,173 --> 01:42:11,466
- You warm enough, miss?
- LlLA: Yes.

1021
01:42:12,468 --> 01:42:14,344
SAM: Did he talk to you?

1022
01:42:15,179 --> 01:42:16,346
No.

1023
01:42:18,182 --> 01:42:23,478
I got the whole story, but not from Norman.
I got it from his mother.

1024
01:42:24,689 --> 01:42:27,190
Norman Bates no longer exists.

1025
01:42:28,151 --> 01:42:30,819
He only half-existed to begin with.

1026
01:42:30,903 --> 01:42:33,822
And now the other half has taken over,

1027
01:42:35,325 --> 01:42:37,409
probably for all time.

1028
01:42:39,537 --> 01:42:41,330
Did he kill my sister?

1029
01:42:41,414 --> 01:42:44,458
Yes. And no.

1030
01:42:45,001 --> 01:42:48,128
Well, now look, if you're trying to lay
some psychiatric groundwork

1031
01:42:48,212 --> 01:42:50,464
for some sort of plea, this fellow would like to cop...

1032
01:42:50,548 --> 01:42:53,425
A psychiatrist doesn't lay the groundwork.

1033
01:42:53,509 --> 01:42:55,844
He merely tries to explain it.

1034
01:42:56,429 --> 01:42:58,680
- But my sister is...
- FRED: Yes.

1035
01:43:01,517 --> 01:43:03,602
Yes, I'm sorry.

1036
01:43:04,687 --> 01:43:06,396
The private investigator, too.

1037
01:43:06,481 --> 01:43:11,026
If you drag that swamp somewhere
in the vicinity of the motel, you'll...

1038
01:43:14,572 --> 01:43:17,658
Have you any unsolved missing
persons cases on your books?

1039
01:43:17,742 --> 01:43:20,369
- Yes, two.
- Young girls?

1040
01:43:21,120 --> 01:43:24,414
- Did he confess to...
- Like I said, the mother.

1041
01:43:26,918 --> 01:43:30,671
Now, to understand it the way I understood
it, hearing it from the mother,

1042
01:43:31,255 --> 01:43:34,508
that is, from the mother half of Norman's mind,

1043
01:43:35,093 --> 01:43:37,511
you have to go back 10 years

1044
01:43:37,970 --> 01:43:41,348
to the time when Norman murdered
his mother and her lover.

1045
01:43:41,432 --> 01:43:46,311
Now, he was already dangerously disturbed,
had been ever since his father died.

1046
01:43:47,355 --> 01:43:50,982
His mother was a clinging, demanding woman,

1047
01:43:52,485 --> 01:43:57,197
and for years the two of them lived
as if there was no one else in the world.

1048
01:43:57,615 --> 01:43:59,408
Then she met a man,

1049
01:43:59,909 --> 01:44:03,745
and it seemed to Norman
that she threw him over for this man.

1050
01:44:04,497 --> 01:44:08,208
Now, that pushed him over the line
and he killed them both.

1051
01:44:08,793 --> 01:44:12,546
Matricide is probably the most
unbearable crime of all,

1052
01:44:14,006 --> 01:44:16,967
most unbearable to the son who commits it.

1053
01:44:17,969 --> 01:44:22,764
So he had to erase the crime,
at least in his own mind.

1054
01:44:25,810 --> 01:44:27,602
He stole her corpse.

1055
01:44:30,148 --> 01:44:32,482
A weighted coffin was buried.

1056
01:44:33,234 --> 01:44:35,819
He hid the body in the fruit cellar,

1057
01:44:36,279 --> 01:44:39,156
even treated it to keep it as well as it would keep.

1058
01:44:39,240 --> 01:44:41,450
And that still wasn't enough.

1059
01:44:42,243 --> 01:44:45,871
She was there, but she was a corpse.

1060
01:44:48,416 --> 01:44:51,209
So he began to think and speak for her,

1061
01:44:51,294 --> 01:44:53,837
give her half his life, so to speak.

1062
01:44:54,464 --> 01:44:58,508
At times he could be both personalities,
carry on conversations.

1063
01:44:59,594 --> 01:45:03,096
At other times,
the mother half took over completely.

1064
01:45:03,556 --> 01:45:08,351
He was never all Norman,
but he was often only Mother,

1065
01:45:09,771 --> 01:45:13,148
and because he was so pathologically jealous of her,

1066
01:45:13,608 --> 01:45:16,067
he assumed that she was as jealous of him.

1067
01:45:16,152 --> 01:45:20,697
Therefore, if he felt a strong
attraction to any other woman,

1068
01:45:21,699 --> 01:45:24,451
the mother side of him would go wild.

1069
01:45:27,747 --> 01:45:29,790
When he met your sister,

1070
01:45:31,167 --> 01:45:33,460
he was touched by her, aroused by her.

1071
01:45:33,544 --> 01:45:35,045
He wanted her.

1072
01:45:37,215 --> 01:45:41,343
That set off the jealous mother,
and Mother killed the girl.

1073
01:45:42,887 --> 01:45:46,598
After the murder,
Norman returned as if from a deep sleep,

1074
01:45:47,183 --> 01:45:51,102
and, like a dutiful son,
covered up all traces of the crime

1075
01:45:51,187 --> 01:45:53,814
he was convinced his mother had committed!

1076
01:45:53,898 --> 01:45:56,233
Why was he dressed like that?

1077
01:45:56,692 --> 01:45:58,568
He's a transvestite.

1078
01:45:59,570 --> 01:46:01,071
Not exactly.

1079
01:46:01,697 --> 01:46:05,116
A man who dresses in women's clothing
in order to achieve a sexual change

1080
01:46:05,201 --> 01:46:07,661
or satisfaction is a transvestite.

1081
01:46:07,745 --> 01:46:12,249
But in Norman's case,
he was simply doing everything possible

1082
01:46:12,333 --> 01:46:15,836
to keep alive the illusion of his mother being alive.

1083
01:46:17,129 --> 01:46:19,673
And when reality came too close,

1084
01:46:19,757 --> 01:46:23,301
when danger or desire threatened that illusion,

1085
01:46:25,638 --> 01:46:28,765
he'd dress up, even to a cheap wig he bought.

1086
01:46:28,850 --> 01:46:32,978
He'd walk about the house,
sit in her chair, speak in her voice.

1087
01:46:33,062 --> 01:46:35,272
He tried to be his mother.

1088
01:46:37,191 --> 01:46:39,860
And now he is.

1089
01:46:41,779 --> 01:46:45,031
That's what I meant when I said
I got the story from the mother.

1090
01:46:45,116 --> 01:46:48,368
You see, when the mind
houses two personalities,

1091
01:46:48,953 --> 01:46:51,621
there's always a conflict, a battle.

1092
01:46:52,582 --> 01:46:55,375
In Norman's case, the battle is over,

1093
01:46:56,210 --> 01:46:58,628
and the dominant personality has won.

1094
01:46:58,713 --> 01:47:01,506
And the $40,000? Who got that?

1095
01:47:02,675 --> 01:47:06,344
The swamp.
These were crimes of passion, not profit.

1096
01:47:08,598 --> 01:47:12,309
He feels a little chill.
Can I bring him this blanket?

1097
01:47:12,393 --> 01:47:14,352
- Oh, sure.
- All right.

1098
01:47:26,741 --> 01:47:28,116
(NORMAN AS MRS. BATES) Thank you.

1099
01:47:35,750 --> 01:47:39,002
MRS. BATES: It's sad when a mother
has to speak the words

1100
01:47:39,086 --> 01:47:41,087
that condemn her own son,

1101
01:47:41,172 --> 01:47:45,008
but I couldn't allow them to believe
that I would commit murder.

1102
01:47:45,092 --> 01:47:48,929
They'll put him away now,
as I should have years ago.

1103
01:47:49,680 --> 01:47:51,765
He was always bad,

1104
01:47:51,849 --> 01:47:56,895
and in the end he intended to tell them
I killed those girls and that man,

1105
01:47:56,979 --> 01:48:00,690
as if I could do anything exceptjust sit and stare,

1106
01:48:00,775 --> 01:48:03,193
like one of his stuffed birds.

1107
01:48:03,945 --> 01:48:07,447
They know I can't even move a finger, and I won't.

1108
01:48:07,531 --> 01:48:13,161
I'll just sit here and be quiet,
just in case they do suspect me.

1109
01:48:16,165 --> 01:48:19,417
They're probably watching me. Well, let them.

1110
01:48:20,002 --> 01:48:22,837
Let them see what kind of a person I am.

1111
01:48:24,048 --> 01:48:26,466
I'm not even gonna swat that fly.

1112
01:48:26,550 --> 01:48:29,302
I hope they are watching. They'll see.

1113
01:48:29,804 --> 01:48:31,471
They'll see and they'll know,

1114
01:48:31,555 --> 01:48:36,059
and they'll say,
"Why, she wouldn't even harm a fly."

